Curtis shrugged the ripped Raira jacket off his shoulder like it had finally finished being useful.
The tear hung open. Takeshi’s trophy. A school color that had already been in too many mouths.
He walked it to the nearest bin, stuffed it in, and dusted his hands once.
“Uniform’s a receipt now,” he muttered. “I’m not wearing a receipt.”
Mikado watched the blue disappear under a convenience-store bag and a crushed can. Then he did the other stupid thing.
He walked up to Celty.
“Um.” His voice went thin and brave at the same time. “Is it true you don’t have a head?”
“Mikado!”
Curtis’s warning came too late to be a wall. It was just a name snapped across a sidewalk.
Celty lifted her phone.
It’s fine.
The yellow helmet came off.
Canon did the rest.
Smoke rose where a face should have been. Not a trick of cloth. Not a shadow from a visor. An absence with weight, black ribbons lifting into late light like the air had learned a new shape. The neck ended. The woman did not.
Mikado’s breath left him in one small sound.
He did not run.
He stared the way a boy stared when the city he had wanted finally looked back and did not blink.
Izaya’s smile went bright and private.
“Well,” he said, almost fond. “Never before seen. And here I thought the rumors were being modest.”
Celty held the helmet at her hip. The PDA came up again, screen turned so Mikado could read it without stepping closer than he already had.
I am a dullahan.
I am looking for my head.
I am not going to hurt you.
Mikado swallowed. His eyes stayed on the smoke.
“…You’re the Headless Rider. For real.” Not a question anymore. “I thought— people say things in Ikebukuro. I didn’t think I’d…” He shook his head once, then looked at her like the lack of a face was less frightening than being lied to. “I’m not scared. I just needed to know if I was walking home with a rumor or a person.”
Curtis stood a half-step off the line they had made, ripped shirt showing where the jacket had been, mismatched eyes hard on Izaya first, then on the first-year who kept handing out truths like they were free samples.
Celty typed.
A person.
Mikado nodded, too fast, like agreement could make the next part safer.
“Then I should tell you. The girl. The one with the scar on her neck. I think that’s who you’ve been chasing. I— I was helping her. I thought if I showed you, we could clear it up.”
Curtis’s jaw ticked.
The bridge had already named Mika. The private line had already named Shinra. This sidewalk did not get to become a second briefing.
He did not say the name.
He said, low, for Mikado and not for the flea:
“You talk about a scar. You do not talk about an address. Not with him smiling.”
Izaya put a hand to his chest, wounded on purpose.
“You make me sound untrustworthy. I’m only here for the extraordinary.” His gaze lingered on the smoke above Celty’s collar the way some men lingered on a rare book. “And it keeps arriving.”
Celty’s visor-less absence turned toward Curtis for a fraction of a second. The private channel stayed shut in front of Izaya. The phone did the public work.
We go.
No speeches on the street.
Mikado looked at the helmet in her hand, then at the empty space that had not become a monster when it was shown to him.
“…Okay.”
He started walking toward home because that was still the shape of Episode 10, even with a foreigner in a torn shirt standing where the plot had not originally budgeted a fourth body.
Curtis fell in on his left.
Celty put the helmet back on before the next corner could grow a crowd.
Izaya followed last, happy as a man who had just watched a myth take off its disguise and a first-year decide that was proof he belonged in the current.
On a sidewalk that had already spent a jacket,
Mikado Ryuugamine asked the question
canon had given him.
Celty Sturluson answered with smoke
and did not become a nightmare.
Izaya Orihara collected the sight
like a jackpot preview.
And Curtis Nightstar threw the ripped coat away so the next room
would not get a school color to grab.